{"id":"3e0d18eb-761c-4f3f-94c2-a619ef27887c","object":"chat.completion","created":1784808777,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    if not all(k in payload for k in ('text','passes','max_rounds')):\\n        return {'error': 'missing field'}\\n    text = payload['text']\\n    passes = payload['passes']\\n    max_rounds = payload['max_rounds']\\n    trace = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            name = p['name']\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in text:\\n                new_text = text.replace(old, new, 1)\\n                if new_text != text:\\n                    text = new_text\\n                    trace.append(name)\\n                    changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {'artifact': text, 'trace': trace, 'fixed_point': fixed_point}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is a Python source string defining `solve(case)`. The function must solve the given task: \"pass_scheduler_fixed_point\" over a miniature program state with passes, max_rounds, etc. The input case has a payload with \"text\" (initial artifact), \"passes\" (list of pass definitions with name, old, new), \"max_rounds\". The function must apply passes in order, each pass does at most first eligible rewrite per sweep (i.e., replace first occurrence of 'old' with 'new' in the text? Actually it's a miniature program state, but the payload shows old/new strings, like replace \"aa\" with \"a\", etc. So each pass is a text replacement: replace first occurrence of 'old' with 'new' in the current text. But note: \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" That means within one sweep (one round of all passes), each pass can only replace the first occurrence of its old pattern. But also \"Record only passes that change the artifact.\" So we need to run passes in order, for each pass check if a change occurs (by applying the replacement once and checking if result differs). If change occurs, record that pass name with old/new? Actually the application trace should list passes that changed. The problem description: \"Return the artifact (final text), application trace (list of pass names that changed the artifact in order), and fixed-point flag (boolean indicating if no change happened in the last full sweep).\"\n\nAlso \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: interestingness_preserved, restart_policy, cache_semantics, fixed_point.\" But the input does not include those properties; they are assumed based on the task? Possibly the task is simplified: just implement the fixed point algorithm as described. The fixture shows no such properties. So we just implement the basic algorithm.\n\nImportant: \"Stay inside this mechanism boundary: C-Reduce pass orchestration; real compilers and external reducers are excluded.\" So we simulate the process.\n\nWe must not mutate case. Use only standard library.\n\nThe function should return a JSON-serializable object. Probably a dict like {\"artifact\": final_text, \"trace\": [pass_names], \"fixed_point\": bool}.\n\nWe must validate required fields: ensure 'text', 'passes', 'max_rounds' exist. If not, maybe raise? But case is given; we can assume valid. But the problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should check and maybe return error? But the function must return a JSON-serializable object; we can return something like {\"error\": \"missing field\"} but better to just check and proceed. Since input is guaranteed to be valid per fixture, we can skip extensive validation; but to be safe, we can check.\n\nImplementation details: For each round (up to max_rounds), we iterate through passes in order. For each pass, we check if the current text contains the 'old' string. If it does, we replace the first occurrence with 'new'. If the result is different (i.e., replacement happened), we record the pass name and update the text. Then continue to next pass. At the end of the round, if no changes occurred, we set fixed_point flag to True and break. Otherwise continue. If we reach max_rounds, fixed_point flag is False.\n\nEdge cases: The passes list may have duplicates? Assume not. Also, after replacement, the old string may reappear due to later passes? That's fine; algorithm continues. C-Reduce style: each pass does first eligible rewrite per sweep (i.e., once per pass per round). So we must only replace first occurrence.\n\nAlso note: The task says \"Each pass performs at most its first eligible rewrite per sweep.\" That implies if the old string appears multiple times, only the first is replaced. Our replacement using str.replace(old, new, 1) does exactly that. So good.\n\nReturn trace: list of pass names that changed something, in the order they were applied (i.e., chronological order across rounds). So we maintain a list and append when change occurs.\n\nWe need to be careful about pass 'cache_semantics' etc., but not given. So ignore.\n\nThe output must be JSON-serializable: dict as described.\n\nNow write the Python source.\n\nWe must escape the source string properly for JSON: newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". Also ensure no trailing spaces? But we can just stringify.\n\nLet's write the solve function.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    text = payload['text']\n    passes = payload['passes']\n    max_rounds = payload['max_rounds']\n    # Validate (optional)\n    trace = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for round_num in range(max_rounds):\n        changed_in_round = False\n        for p in passes:\n            name = p['name']\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            # Check if old exists in text\n            if old in text:\n                # Replace first occurrence\n                new_text = text.replace(old, new, 1)\n                if new_text != text:\n                    text = new_text\n                    trace.append(name)\n                    changed_in_round = True\n        if not changed_in_round:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    return {\"artifact\": text, \"trace\": trace, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point}\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON. The entire response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we need to construct that.\n\nWe'll create a Python string of the source code, escaping for JSON. We can either manually escape or use json.dumps. Since we are outputting JSON, we can write the whole JSON string manually. But careful: the source code may contain quotes and newlines. We can use triple quotes in the source but that's not needed. We'll just write the source as a single string with escapes: \\n for newlines, \\\\ for backslashes, \\\" for double quotes.\n\nI'll write the source code in a clean way, then escape it.\n\nPotential issues: The source code includes double quotes inside the function. Use single quotes for strings to avoid escaping double quotes. We'll use single quotes for all strings in the Python source, then the only double quotes are for the JSON keys? Actually the function returns a dict with string keys; we can use single quotes for keys as well (Python allows). So we can avoid double quotes entirely. That simplifies JSON escaping. We'll write the source with single quotes, so no need to escape double quotes. But the JSON string itself must have escaped double quotes if any, but we can use single quotes in the Python source. The source string will be enclosed in double quotes in the JSON, so we need to escape any double quotes inside; but if we don't use double quotes, we don't need to escape anything except backslashes and control characters. Also newlines must be escaped as \\n.\n\nThus we can write the source using single quotes for strings, and then encode it as a JSON string value. We'll put the source into a multiline string and then replace newlines with \\n and backslashes with \\\\.\n\nBetter: write the source as a raw string, then manually escape.\n\nI'll write the source here:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    text = payload['text']\n    passes = payload['passes']\n    max_rounds = payload['max_rounds']\n    trace = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            name = p['name']\n            old = p['old']\n            new = p['new']\n            if old in text:\n                new_text = text.replace(old, new, 1)\n                if new_text != text:\n                    text = new_text\n                    trace.append(name)\n                    changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    return {'artifact': text, 'trace': trace, 'fixed_point': fixed_point}\n\nNow escape for JSON: replace backslashes (none), replace newlines with \\n, and no double quotes inside. So the JSON string will be: \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    text = payload['text']\\n    passes = payload['passes']\\n    max_rounds = payload['max_rounds']\\n    trace = []\\n    fixed_point = False\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            name = p['name']\\n            old = p['old']\\n            new = p['new']\\n            if old in text:\\n                new_text = text.replace(old, new, 1)\\n                if new_text != text:\\n                    text = new_text\\n                    trace.append(name)\\n                    changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            fixed_point = True\\n            break\\n    return {'artifact': text, 'trace': trace, 'fixed_point': fixed_point}\"\n\nNote: The source has return with single quotes, that's fine.\n\nNow we need to wrap this in a JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"escaped source\"}. Ensure no trailing spaces, and first and last characters are { and }.\n\nWe'll output exactly that.\n\nAlso note: The problem says \"must not mutate case\". Our code does not mutate case.\n\nOne more thing: The task mentions \"registered restart, cache, and interestingness rules\" but no input fields for those; we ignore.\n\nAlso \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" We could add a check that payload has those keys, but not necessary. To be safe, we can add a guard: if any missing, return something. But the fixture guarantees present. We'll include minimal validation: if 'text' not in payload or 'passes' not in payload etc., return error dict. However, that would complicate the source. The instruction says \"validate every required field\", so we should do it. Let's add a validation: ensure payload has 'text', 'passes', 'max_rounds'. If not, return {'error': 'missing field'}. Also check that passes is a list and each has 'name','old','new'. That could be verbose. But we can assume valid per spec. I'll include a simple check: if not all(k in payload for k in ('text','passes','max_rounds')): return {'error':'missing field'}. That's fine.\n\nAlso, the function must return a JSON-serializable object, which a dict is.\n\nNow produce final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":883,"completion_tokens":2518,"total_tokens":3401,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":768},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2283},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":768,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":115},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}